<Base title="About" description="About Yu: someone who takes things apart, understands them and remakes them.">
  <article class="wrap page">
    <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
    <h1 class="page-title">Yu.</h1>
    <div class="prose">
      <p class="lede">I take apart off-the-shelf products and existing systems, work out how they behave, and remake them for what I need. Where I dig deepest is industrial 3D printing.</p>
      <p>
        I like working through materials, slicer settings and firmware from the physics up — heat, flow and cooling.
        What I learn, I turn into tools and guides that other people can use too.
      </p>

      <h2>What I do.</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Dialling in FDM / FFF print settings, and tracking down the causes of print failures</li>
        <li>Slicer settings and profile conversion (<a href="/en/research/">Research</a>)</li>
        <li>Modifying and building 3D printers, mostly around Klipper</li>
        <li>Building tools that keep work moving (MCP servers, automation, AI agents)</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How I work.</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Measure before optimising. Use things as they are first, and build a system only around the friction that keeps coming back.</li>
        <li>Make changes in stages.</li>
        <li>Curiosity and the fun of it come first; usefulness follows.</li>
        <li>The only comparison that matters is with my past self.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Languages.</h2>
      <p>Japanese, English, Korean.</p>

      <h2>The name yunoxia.</h2>
      <p>
        It's the name of this site and of what I do. It's a coined word: "yu" (you / <span lang="ja">優</span> / <span lang="ja">遊</span>) + "no" + "quxia".
        "quxia" comes from a language that doesn't exist — a word with meaning but no intent behind it.
      </p>

      <h2>Contact.</h2>
      <p><Fragment set:html="<!--email_off-->" /><a href="mailto:contact@yunoxia.one">contact@yunoxia.one</a><Fragment set:html="<!--/email_off-->" /></p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>

